## API

### Orders API

| Priority | Tags | Steps |
|---|---|---|
| P2 | #api | 31 |

**Steps**

1. ✔ Send POST /oauth/token (save as token) — Get a token
2. ✔ Send POST /api/{{env.api-version}}/orders (save as order) — Create an order
3. Read the location header of the response (save as location)
4. Read the response status (save as code)
5. Send GET /admin — Basic auth
6. Send GET /reports — API key in query
7. Send DELETE /api/orders/{{vars.order.id}} — API key header
8. Send GET /me — Cookie
9. ✔ Send GET /x — OAuth2 client credentials
10. Send POST /files — Upload
11. Send PUT https://other.test/raw — Raw text
12. ✔ Send a GraphQL query to /graphql — GraphQL
13. If {{vars.code}} equals "201"
   - Note: Created {{vars.order.id}}
   - Otherwise:
      - Note: Not created
14. Repeat 3 times
   - Send GET /ping?i={{vars.index}}
15. Repeat for each line in {{vars.order.items}}
   - Note: {{vars.line.sku}}
16. Repeat while the "Load more" button is visible
   - Note: more
17. If "Out of stock" is on the page
   - Note: oos
18. Repeat while {{vars.index}} lt "3" — Poll until three
   - Send GET /poll
19. Remember "[object Object]" as payload
20. Generate a random pattern (save as ref)
21. Generate a random date (save as due)
22. Wait 250 ms
23. Run a script (save as double)


### Integrations

| Priority | Tags | Steps |
|---|---|---|
| P2 | #api | 7 |

**Steps**

1. ✔ Call the SOAP service /soap/prices (urn:GetPrice) (save as price) — Get the price
2. ✔ Call the SOAP service https://soap.example.test/ws (urn:Ping) — SOAP 1.2 envelope
3. ✔ Send a Kafka message to orders with key "{{vars.ref}}" — Publish an order event
4. ✔ Wait for a Kafka message on invoices where $.orderId is "{{vars.ref}}" (save as invoice) — Wait for the invoice event
5. Upload /in/prices-{{run.id}}.csv on SFTP files.shop.test — Upload the price list
6. ✔ Download /out/report.csv on FTP files.shop.test (save as report) — Read the report
7. ✔ List /in on FTPS files.shop.test — List the inbox


## Back office

### Data and email

| Priority | Tags | Steps |
|---|---|---|
| P3 | — | 20 |

**Steps**

1. ✔ Run the SQL query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM orders WHERE email = $1" on main (save as counts) — Order count
2. Read value from the query result (save as n)
3. Run a SQL script on local
4. Call the procedure refresh_totals
5. Run the SQL query "SELECT 1" on legacy
6. ✔ Find documents in orders on docs (save as docs)
7. ✔ Run the SQL query "{ "collection": "orders", "countDocuments": { "email": "{{data.email}}" } }" on docs — Count orders in Mongo
8. Check data quality on main
9. Run the SQL query "SELECT ?" on unknown
10. Wait for an email to {{data.email}} with subject "Your order" (save as mail)
11. ✔ Check that the subject contains "order" and the body contains "Thank you" and it comes from shop@ and it has a link and it has the attachment "invoice"
12. Read the one-time code from the email (save as otp)
13. Read the "track" link from the email (save as trackUrl)
14. Read "Order (\w+)" from the email (save as orderNo)
15. Open the "track" link in the email
16. Wait for an email with subject "Welcome"
17. Measure page performance (LCP, CLS, TTFB)
18. Run Lighthouse on /
19. Load test /api/orders with 10 users (load)
20. Check the query plan of "SELECT 1"

